# keri-bench — Intent Tree (this.i)
#
# Source of truth for keri-bench's intentions and the decisions that follow from them
# (~/code/bakobo/dev/methodology.md). Each consequential decision is recorded here, in its own commit,
# before the code that implements it. Ids are minted by `i id` and never typed.
#
# Origin: the KERI-006 speculative interview, held 2026-10-15, recorded at
# bakobo/utah-keri-adoption design/bench-design/interview.md (branch bench-design).

Measure end-to-end KERI+ACDC issuance on any implementation, well enough to buy hardware on = goal:
  nid: 4y4rrghd
  why: >
    The State of Utah's KERI-006 asks for "Load and soak tests covering throughput, persistence,
    concurrency, restart recovery, witnessing, TEL replay, and wallet delivery", and its test-plan
    note says the numbers feed an HSM or accelerator purchase and must "measure end-to-end issuance,
    not simply the time required to generate an ACDC or compute a Merkle root". It names seven pairs
    the plan must keep apart. Chose a new stack-agnostic repo over load tests inside imbu, because
    Utah must be able to rerun the same instrument against KERIA or any other implementation
    (R-VQRD, 2026-10-14), and numbers produced by the vendor's own test suite are weaker evidence.
    Tradeoff: an adapter layer and a fault proxy that imbu-internal tests would not need.
  children:
    It is a measurement instrument, not a pass/fail suite = goal:
      nid: 9jhurd2v
      why: >
        Its job is numbers a purchase decision and an independent rerun can both trust; pass/fail
        is a thin later layer (plan item bench-targets) applied when Utah supplies targets. Rejected
        a suite with thresholds now, because Utah has given none and a pass against an invented
        target is worse than a number. Tradeoff: nothing turns red on its own until targets exist.
        Daniel confirmed, 2026-10-15 (Q1).
      children:
        Report distributions under open-loop load, with the driver checking itself = decision:
          nid: 808tr3fn
          why: >
            Percentiles from a histogram, with offered and achieved load, never a mean alone. Load
            arrives on a schedule independent of completions, because a closed loop hides queueing
            delay (coordinated omission), which is exactly what a saturated issuance lock produces.
            A run whose driver was the bottleneck is refused rather than reported. Tradeoff: a more
            complex driver than a simple request loop.
          children:
            The calibration stack is real processes behind the proxy, with injected delays logged as ground truth = decision:
              nid: 6he49n0k
              why: >-
                Part B #1 must show the harness measures correctly, so the synthetic stack is four small HTTP processes (issuer, state distribution, holder, verifier) started through process control, reached only through the fault proxy, and driven by synthetic adapters that use every observation path a real adapter uses: observed at an API boundary and at the proxy, polled, and reported on a stack clock deliberately skewed from wall time so the offset measurement is exercised. Each process draws every injected delay from a seeded log-normal, logs it to a truth file, and serializes construction behind a fixed service rate, so the throughput cap is known. The calibration compares the harness's report against the truth files. Rejected fake adapters that emit their injected delays directly into the timeline, because that would test arithmetic on numbers the harness never measured. Rejected a sleep-only in-process fake, because it would skip process control, the proxy and the clock offsets, which are where measurement error comes from. Tradeoff: the calibration takes tens of seconds in CI, and its tolerances must absorb loopback round trips (about 0.2 ms through the proxy), so injected delays are tens of milliseconds.
              children:
                ? CI gates the calibration on what load cannot change; the two-sided bar is held on a recorded run and on the bench host = decision
                : nid: 0hykugwr
                  why: >-
                    The live calibration held each interval's p99 to 5% of the truth in both directions, and on GitHub's shared runners that failed about one run in ten: run 42097193536 (main, 702f41a, 2026-11-05) failed when one of three repetitions measured delivered->stored 7.5 ms and proof_generated->verified 43 ms above the truth at p99, against tolerances of 5.2 and 6.3 ms, its p50s within 0.43 ms, on a commit that changed only the witnesses' map size; main failed twice more on 2026-10-21, logs since expired. The truth is what elapsed inside a role's own process, so the excess is the harness's observation path (the round trip through the proxy, three processes waiting on a busy host's scheduler), real time a loaded host adds and the harness correctly reports. The upper side of the bar measures the host, not the harness. So the calibration has two gates. The bounds gate checks only what load cannot move: every injected transition is measured for every credential; each credential's measured interval plus its resolution (which includes both ends' offset bounds) is at least what had elapsed inside the role when it began the effect that ends the transition, because every observation brackets that much of the role's work (the truth's floor_ns: for a transition a role ends by calling another role, the full elapsed time also counts the answer's way back, which the harness stamps earlier at the proxy, and on kelun that made the measured interval up to 3.5 ms shorter than elapsed_ns); each measured offset to a skewed stack clock agrees with the realtime offset less the injected skew within the two offsets' bounds; the stall's credential reaches the stall's length; and the saturation detector fires, at no more than the cap plus 10%, since the issuer cannot construct faster than its service rate. A lower bound alone would pass a harness that adds a constant to everything, so the strict gate keeps the two-sided 5% (p50, p99), the saturation within 10% both ways and the stall's median below half the stall, and it runs in two places: in CI on a calibration recorded on kelun, whose timelines, truth files and scenarios are committed and whose reports CI rebuilds with the code under test, so the analysis is held to the full bar with nothing timed; and live on the bench host before every run of record, as before, where a failure says the host is unfit to measure on. Every calibration writes its scenarios beside its runs, so any calibration can be replayed. Rejected retrying the live check: it hides the same flake at a lower rate and makes a real regression look like noise. Rejected widening the tolerance: any width a loaded runner never crosses is too wide to say anything on a quiet host. Rejected dropping the live run from CI: the bounds gate is what still exercises process control, the proxy and the clock offsets on every change. Tradeoff: a regression that adds a small constant delay to the live observation path is caught on the bench host, not in CI, and the recording must be captured again when the timeline schema or the calibration scenarios change. A run is still refused for driver lag (808tr3fn), so a host loaded enough to refuse every repetition fails the bounds gate for having measured nothing. Daniel chose determinism over a retry, 2026-11-17 (Q-K1MG).
                  children:
                    The strict gate's interval tolerance has a 5 ms floor = decision:
                      nid: 93uwk6r3
                      why: >-
                        A transition the harness observes itself counts one HTTP round trip through the adapter, the proxy and the role, and on kelun that adds 1.2 to 3.2 ms at p50 (0.1 to 0.4 ms for transitions the stack reports on its own clock). The injected medians are 60 to 100 ms, so 5% of them is 3 to 5 ms, and a busy kelun failed the strict gate on that constant alone: 3.20 ms against 3.16 on 2026-11-17, and 3.4 ms against 3.2 at bench-stock-2's first calibration, which was rerun until it passed (docs/stock-2026-11.md). So the tolerance is the largest of 5% of the truth, the interval's resolution and 5 ms. The intervals runs of record report are hundreds of milliseconds and longer (requested to witnessed p50 355 ms at three homes), where 5 ms is under 1.5%; the error budget still reports each transition's measured error in ns, so the floor loosens the pass mark, not what a reader sees. Rejected lengthening the injected delays about five-fold: it keeps the 5% wording but hides that each observation has a fixed cost, and lengthens the calibration. Tradeoff: an interval under 100 ms in a run of record is held to a looser relative bar than 5%, and nobody has audited every run of record for one. Daniel chose the 5 ms floor, 2026-11-17 (Q-MWHR).
            'The driver holds a core: it yields to the event loop continuously instead of sleeping between sends = decision':
              nid: 0pqdn6ne
              why: >-
                Measured on kelun, 2026-10-15, at 20 sends per second against the calibration stack: sleeping until each send gave a p99 send lag of 5 to 22 ms, because a thread that blocks in epoll is woken late by the OS on a shared box (and asyncio rounds epoll timeouts to whole milliseconds); sleeping until 10 ms before each send and then yielding gave 2 to 5 ms, still refused now and then; yielding throughout gave 0.2 to 0.35 ms, even under nice 19. A run whose driver lags is refused (808tr3fn), so an imprecise driver throws away runs the stack did nothing to deserve. Rejected a driver thread with its own loop: it would timestamp sends precisely while the requests themselves still left late from the busy main loop, hiding exactly the lag the self-check exists to catch. Tradeoff: the harness uses one core for the whole window, which the report states as harness CPU, and on a small host that core is taken from the stack under test.
        Every number is re-derivable from a raw timeline and carries full provenance = decision:
          nid: 8q4myzru
          why: >
            Each run writes a versioned JSONL timeline of events (one line per event, naming every
            credential it covers, so one anchor or batch for many credentials is one line) and a
            report derived from it and the run's provenance, plus component commits, versions, policy knobs, host fingerprint and the
            scenario file. A disputed number can then be recomputed rather than argued. Rejected
            reporting only aggregates, because an aggregate cannot be audited. Tradeoff: large run
            directories.
          children:
            ? A role process that exits when the harness did not stop it is recorded in the timeline and the provenance, and the run is reported degraded = decision
            : nid: 9zgpyjxt
              why: >-
                In imbu-prj's traced 40,000 fill on heti 3a8b1ccb (2026-11-03, ~/code/bakobo/heti/.ignored/witness-kel-growth-2-findings.md, finding 2), one witness exited at 05:07 and the fill fell from 1.66 to 0.19 a second, yet keri-bench wrote nothing: its timeline and report described a three-witness stack that by then had two, and the exit code was lost for good (keri-bench tick ~7gec). So the process controller waits on every process it launches, and when one exits without the harness having killed, restarted or stopped it, writes a process_exited event with the process's name, pid, exit code (negative for a signal, as asyncio gives it) and wall time, on the harness clock; the run copies every such exit into provenance.json as unexpected_exits; and the report, derived from the timeline, marks the run degraded with a warning naming each exit. Degraded, not refused: a refused run says its numbers describe nothing, while these numbers describe a stack that lost a role at a known moment, which is what the 3a8b1ccb run's outage measured by accident and is worth keeping, so the reader is told rather than the run discarded. Exits the harness causes, a crash fault's kill and restart and the teardown, are not unexpected and are not recorded as such. Rejected restarting the role automatically: a supervisor that heals would hide the very thing a measurement must show. Tradeoff: a process that dies while the harness is between deciding to stop it and signalling it is counted as intended; the window is microseconds and only at teardown.
        Soak reports drift against accumulated state, not only elapsed time = decision:
          nid: 4xtyfdd0
          why: >
            Persistence costs grow with the store (LMDB size, registry count; the SEDI pool target is
            about 12M registries), so degradation is plotted against credentials issued so far and
            database size. Rejected a fixed-duration soak with one before/after number, because it
            cannot tell time-driven from size-driven decay.
          children:
            ? Drift is an ordinary least-squares slope over consecutive buckets of credentials, against store count, elapsed time and bytes on disk, with a Student t interval = decision
            : nid: 7f9jfc99
              why: >-
                The test plan defines drift as the slope of p50 and p99 per 10^k credentials with a 95% confidence interval, and pairs soak with soak-preseeded so that size-driven decay can be told from time-driven decay. So a soak run's credentials, in the order they were requested, are cut into consecutive buckets of a declared size; each bucket gives a p50 and a p99 of the run's measure interval through the one named percentile definition; and each is regressed on three positions of the bucket's midpoint: credentials the store held (the preseed count plus those requested before it), seconds since the window opened, and bytes the issuer's store occupied, sampled during the run. The slope's 95% interval is the t interval with n-2 degrees of freedom, so it is honest at the few dozen buckets a soak on one box yields, where a normal interval would be too narrow. Every bucket's numbers go into the report, so a slope can be recomputed or refitted. Rejected a bootstrap interval: it needs a seed to be re-derivable and says nothing a t interval does not when the residuals are well-behaved, and the bucket table lets anyone check that. Rejected regressing raw per-credential latencies: one stalled credential would dominate, and a percentile per bucket is what the plan asks about. Tradeoff: bucket size trades resolution for noise, so it is a declared part of the scenario.
            ? A preseeded store is filled by issuing through the same stack before the window opens, and a soak stops on completions or time = decision
            : nid: 9yx6cg92
              why: >-
                soak-preseeded starts from a store pre-filled to size S (docs/test-plan.md, Soak). The store is filled by the issuer adapter issuing S credentials, issue stage only, with a declared number in flight, before the measured window, so the preseeded store holds exactly what this stack writes for a credential: its registry, its TEL and KEL events, its operation records, its witnesses' receipts, and the Registrar's and Observer's copies. Preseeded credentials carry no requested event, so no interval or throughput counts them, and the preseed's count, failures and duration are recorded as harness events. Rejected copying a prepared store into place: every role's store would have to agree with every other's, and a store built by anything but the stack is a claim about the stack nobody checked. A soak's [stop] ends the open-loop schedule at whichever comes first of a declared number of credentials that reached the measure's end milestone or a declared duration, and the timeline records which stopped it. Counting completions rather than requests makes the credential stop mean the store grew by that much. Tradeoff: preseeding costs S divided by the stack's saturated rate in wall time before every preseeded run.
        The October 2026 baseline's sizes on kelun, chosen from probes of today's imbu = decision:
          nid: 82r8lxy5
          why: >-
            The brief for bench-baseline leaves N, K, the load grid, the soak's stop and duration and the preseed size to this run, sized to what kelun (8 cores, 23 GB, shared with other workers) can sustain. Probes on 2026-10-20 at harness 7c3865d: one home issuing only, requested to published, kept up at 1 per second offered, reached 1.23 anchored per second at 2 offered, and collapsed to 0.56 at 5 offered as operations hit their 60 s timeout; three homes kept up at 4 per second with p50 0.75 s; five homes collapsed at 5 per second (accepted 4.8, anchored 2.8), so past three homes the shared witnesses, Registrar and box CPU, not the homes, set the ceiling. Hence N = 3, the most homes this box serves before the shared infrastructure saturates first, and K = 4 clients per home against K = 1. issue-steady issues only and measures requested to published, the issuer side end to end through witnessing and state distribution, so the holder and verifier, one process each, cannot become the bottleneck that N homes is meant to find. The grid steps to saturation: 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5 and 2 per second at one home, 60 s each; 1 to 6 per second at three homes, 45 s each, since more samples arrive at higher rates; 10 s warmup and 120 s drain everywhere, three repetitions per step. Restart runs the four-stage lifecycle to verified at about half of saturation (0.6 per second at one home, 2 at three), crashing at 20 s of a 60 s window: the issuer (every home at one home, home h0 alone at three, to show one home's crash does not stop the others), one witness of three at toad 2, the whole witness pool, state distribution, the holder and the verifier. Soak issues only at about 60% of saturation (0.7 per second at one home, 2.4 at three) for 30 minutes or 1200 and 4000 credentials, buckets of 50, the store sampled every 30 s; soak-preseeded starts from S equal to those stop counts, preseeded 2 and 6 in flight, so the preseeded run starts where the plain soak ends and the two can be compared at equal store size and at equal elapsed time. The Registrar window is 2 s, not the 900 s default, so published measures the stack rather than the window (6f6he77q). The driver's lag limit is 25 ms for these scenarios, not the plan's 5 ms: the probes' refusals were p99 send lags of 6 to 12 ms at three homes, a nice-19 driver preempted by other workers' normal-priority processes on a shared box, while the shortest interval measured here is about 700 ms, so a 25 ms lag is under 4% of it and cannot set the pace; latency still runs from the scheduled send, so lag only ever adds to it. Rejected running the driver at a higher priority than the stack: the box rule is nice 19 for everything this worker starts. Tradeoff: these are numbers for a shared host, and the report says so.
          children:
            ? "Corrections after the baseline's first pass: reconcile a few at a time, crash at a rate the lifecycle sustains, and run more repetitions where the host refuses them = decision"
            : nid: 2hn3mn3j
              why: >-
                The first pass of the matrix (2026-10-20, harness fc670cc) was stopped and rerun whole on one corrected harness, because three of its results were the harness's, not imbu's. First, reconciliation asked 32 status questions at once; on this stack each makes the one holder present again and the one verifier admit and verify, so they timed out and up to 30 of 36 credentials that had reached verified were counted lost. It now asks 4 at a time, asks a failed question twice more a second apart, calls a credential the verifier never answered unanswered rather than lost, and asks nothing in a run that never verified, where no credential has a fate to reconcile. Second, at three homes the crash scenarios offered 2 per second through the whole lifecycle, which overloads the one holder and the one verifier the homes share: p50 to verified was 16 s or more before every crash and about 80 s after, so the runs measured a queue rather than recovery. Every crash scenario now offers 0.6 per second at both topologies, where one home's lifecycle ran at p50 2.6 to 4 s, so the shared holder and verifier carry the same load at one home and at three and a restart is measured against normal load. Third, the driver check refused 15 of 18 three-home throughput runs on send lag of 27 to 190 ms while other workers' tests held cores at the same nice level; a refused run says nothing about the stack, so a sweep now keeps running repetitions until three are accepted or six have been made, and every refused run stays in the record. Rejected raising the lag limit again: at 100 ms or more the driver could set the pace of a 700 ms interval, which is exactly what the check exists to catch. Tradeoff: on a busy host a step can still end with fewer than three accepted runs, and the report says how many it has.
        ? "bench-targets judges imbu utah/next 670a3d8 against Utah's stated targets, each with the evidence that bears on it, at 1 home and at 3 = decision"
        : nid: 4j5395l8
          why: >-
            Utah answered on 2026-10-25 (plan item utah-answers, private correspondence, so stated here in our words): at most 10,000 credentials a day; up to a million SEDI credentials in a decade for Utah and more for larger coalition states; a revocation must reach verifiers within 30 minutes; no crash-recovery target yet; and a Thales network HSM chosen to keep keys from State staff rather than for speed, doing thousands of signatures a second. The PM's brief (2026-10-27) turns those into five judgements, and this node says how each is measured, reusing the baselines' method, harness and host. Volume: the peak-hour target assumes all 10,000 arrive in one hour, 2.8 a second, and the office-day target spreads them over 8 hours, 0.35 a second; each is a soak at that rate until 10,000 credentials completed or its hour or eight hours ran out, measured requested to witnessed, judged on p50 and p99 and on whether achieved throughput kept up, at one home and at three. A soak rather than a 60 s sweep step, because the first baseline showed latency growing with every credential, so a short window at the target rate would pass a stack that could not issue the day's 10,000. Stock: soak and soak-preseeded at the largest preseed this host's disk and hours allow, at one home, because the growth the first baseline found is per issuer (each home's KEL and outbox), so one home is the worst case for a given stock and three homes would hide it; latency, bytes on disk and restart time are fitted against stock and extrapolated to a million, saying what was extrapolated from what; the 963dfec curve comes from the first baseline's committed soaks rather than a rerun, since imbu's publication then is the same at 8512c1d. Revocation: at the peak-hour rate, a sample of credentials runs the whole lifecycle and is revoked (8654nxrl), with the Registrar's default 900 s window, the verifier's freshness at 900 s so that window plus freshness is the 30 minutes, and the Observer resynchronizing at three windows as the summit harness sets it; revocation_requested to revocation_recognised is reported as a distribution against 1800 s. Crash recovery: the baseline's twelve crash scenarios rerun at this imbu, reported without a verdict. HSM: signatures per credential from the census with and without Prefer: wait (5k8z0pxe) times the target rate, against the device's thousands a second, confirmed by one run at a network HSM's latency at the target rate rather than a new sweep. The load is dealt to 4 clients per home, because imbu's answer budget is per controller and a Drivers License Division has many offices calling it; the first baseline showed K does not move a home's capacity. Rejected judging the volume target from the baselines' sweep steps: they ran 60 s from an empty store. Rejected a million-credential live issuance: at this host's rates it takes days, and the fitted curve answers where the wall is. Tradeoff: one long run per volume target, so between-run spread is not measured, as for the baselines' soaks.
          children:
            "A scenario may run its later stages on a sample of credentials, every Nth, and issue the rest only = decision":
              nid: 8654nxrl
              why: >-
                The revocation target is measured at the volume target, and every stage after issue runs through the one holder and one verifier, which the first baseline found saturate near 0.6 lifecycles a second, far below 2.8, while Utah says revocation of SEDI credentials will be rare. So a scenario may say [lifecycle] full_every = N: the credential the driver schedules at index i runs every stage the scenario names when i is a multiple of N, and only issue otherwise; without the table every credential runs every stage, as before. The measured interval and throughput still count every credential; per-pair intervals count only those that reached both milestones, which the report already does. N is a positive integer, refused otherwise with a coded error, and is part of the scenario's identity. Rejected a second, separately scheduled stream of revoked credentials: the sample would then add load the volume target does not name, and the harness drives one schedule. Rejected sampling at random: a seed would be needed to re-derive which credentials ran, and every Nth already spreads them evenly across the window. Tradeoff: the sample is the scheduled order's, so a pattern with period N in the stack would bias it, which nothing in imbu's round-robin dealing has unless N shares a factor with homes times clients; the targets' N is chosen prime to it.
            "The targets run's sizes on kelun, chosen from probes of imbu 670a3d8 = decision":
              nid: 9cuqzk0u
              why: >-
                Probes on 2026-10-27 at harness 4a328f0, issuance only, a waiting client, 4 clients a home, the deployed 900 s window, 60 s windows, one run a step (results/targets-2026-10/probes): one home kept up at 1 and 1.5 a second (p50 requested to witnessed 0.38 and 0.49 s) and collapsed at 2 (1.29 witnessed a second) and 2.8 (0.9, operations past their 60 s timeout); three homes kept up at 2.8 and 4 (p50 0.38 and 0.42 s) and saturated at 5 (4.1). The capacities are the baselines', so publication without the KEL did not move a home's ceiling from an empty store. A home's store grew about 17 KB a credential, where the first baseline's grew to 1 MB a credential by the 1200th. So: peak-hour-1home runs 10 minutes, not the hour, because one home fails 2.8 a second from its first minute and an hour of collapse says nothing more; peak-hour-3homes runs the full hour with every 101st credential (prime to 3 homes times 4 clients) through the lifecycle and revoked, a 40-minute drain so the last sample's two windows can pass, the verifier retrying every 5 s for up to 20 minutes and recognition polled every 10 s, which is 0.6% of the 30-minute target; office-day-1home runs the full eight hours, and office-day-3homes runs last, as a confirmation, since each of its homes carries a third of the rate and stock the one-home run does while the shared roles carry the same total. Stock: the office day's one home grows from empty to 10,000, and stock-preseeded-1home starts from 40,000, preseeded 4 in flight, which at about 1.5 a second is seven to eight hours and about 0.7 GB at 17 KB a credential, then soaks 30 minutes at the office rate and kills the issuer at 900 s, so latency, bytes and restart time are read at two stocks well apart and the curve is fitted over both (keri-bench growth) to the million. Rejected a preseed of 100,000 or more: about 18 hours of the shared host before the window opens, when the fit's question, linear or quadratic, is settled by 0 to 10,000 and one point at 40,000. HSM: 15 ms per signature, the grid's floor on this host (63txu3fm), standing for a network HSM in the same data centre, whose few milliseconds this host cannot honour; three 120 s repetitions at 2.8 a second at three homes, waiting, polling with the budget out of the way, and polling within imbu's default budget. Crash recovery: the baseline's twelve scenarios with only the issuer's knobs changed, so the baseline's 2 s window and 0.6 a second stay, and the numbers compare with the baseline's. Tradeoff: one run each for the volume and stock targets, read with their buckets, as the baselines' soaks were.
              children:
                ? "Revocation freshness is measured on the office day at three homes, because at the peak rate neither issuance nor the Observer keeps up = decision"
                : nid: 2p4c5jhw
                  why: >-
                    peak-hour-3homes, run on 2026-10-27 from 02:07 UTC at harness d09bde9, collapsed after about 1,800 credentials: anchored to witnessed rose from 0.12 s to 0.5 s as each home's KEL grew, then accepting to anchoring queued past imbu's 60 s timeout, and each home held about 1,980 pending operations. Separately, heti's Observer refused all three of the Registrar's 900 s batches it was sent, each about 2,500 heads, because judging a batch has a 120 s bound (heti observer/service.py BATCH_JUDGE_TIMEOUT) and asking the witnesses about an issuer ran past it; it resubscribed each time, so no sampled credential ever verified and none was revoked. Revocation at 2.8 a second therefore cannot be measured on this stack, and the report says so rather than giving a number. The sample moves to office-day-3homes, the other form of the volume target: every 101st credential through the lifecycle and revoked, about 99 revocations over eight hours, with the same window, freshness, polling and drain as the peak run, so the 900 s batches carry about 315 heads. office-day-1home stays issuance only, so the target 1 judgement and the stock curve at one home carry no lifecycle load. Rejected a revocation run at a lower peak, such as 2 a second: Utah's target is stated per day, and the office day is the volume it asked about that this stack can carry. Tradeoff: the revocation figure is at 0.35 a second, and the report must say it was not measured at the peak.
                "The stock run preseeds 10,000 and soaks at 0.1 a second, because issuance time grows with the issuer's KEL = decision":
                  nid: 0kc32dlp
                  why: >-
                    office-day-1home, run 2026-10-27 04:40 to 12:42 UTC at harness dab9469, witnessed credentials 0 to 4,035 and then none of the remaining 6,044: each credential's accepted-to-published time grew about 0.6 ms for every credential the home already held (p50 0.35 s + 0.61 ms x n over 0 to 4,000, R2 0.97), and when it reached the 2.86 s between arrivals the home fell behind, imbu answered 401 e.id.expired.signature.f to 5,063 later requests whose signatures aged in its queue, and nothing more was witnessed. Store bytes grew linearly, 13.2 KB a credential (R2 0.9999; the quadratic term is 0.03 bytes, against 413 at 963dfec). The preseed issues one credential at a time through the same path, so filling a store to S costs about 0.55 S + 0.0003 S^2 seconds: 40,000 would take about five and a half days, not the 7 to 8 hours 9cuqzk0u assumed from a constant rate, so the 40,000 run was stopped in its first minutes (runs/targets-aborted). The preseed becomes 10,000, the volume target's one day at one home, about 10 hours with 4 in flight and each operation near 25 s at the end, inside imbu's 60 s timeout. The window's rate becomes 0.1 a second, below the about 0.15 a home at 10,000 sustains, for 1,800 s with 30-credential buckets and the issuer still killed at 900 s, so the window measures latency and restart at that stock rather than a collapse. office-day-3homes runs first, since it carries targets 1 and 3 and each home holds about 3,360 credentials, well under where 0.117 a second per home tips (about 14,000). Rejected 20,000 (about 35 hours) and keeping 40,000 at 0.35 a second (the window would collapse at once and say only that). Tradeoff: stock beyond 10,000 in one issuer's KEL is extrapolated from two runs, 0 to 4,000 and 10,000, and the report must say so; the million is extrapolated further still, and how it divides among issuers decides whether any one KEL ever gets there.
        ? bench-targets-2 reruns targets 1 to 5 against imbu utah/next 2c3b072 and heti 1ae3590 with bench-targets' scenarios, method and sizes, changing only the pins and the issuer adapter = decision
        : nid: 5frsx7pu
          why: >-
