assing less of heti's loop: the port and advertised URL are chosen inside it, so the copy stays until heti takes them as parameters. Tradeoff: the copy can drift from heti's loop again; the test that the bench witnesses' stores are mapped at heti's size catches the store half of that drift, not the rest.
    The harness owns the lifecycle vocabulary; adapters report against it, per role = goal:
      nid: 7wt73dlu
      why: >
        The seven pairs define fixed milestones (requested, constructed, registry updated, anchored,
        witnessed, published, delivered, stored, proof generated, verified, revocation requested /
        anchored / witnessed / published / recognised, verifier synced full and incremental). A stack
        does not get to redefine "done". Rejected per-stack metrics, because KERIA's "operation done"
        and imbu's synchronous return mean different things, and Utah could not compare them. Adapters
        are per role (issuer, witness pool, state distribution, holder, verifier) so a run can mix a
        KERIA issuer with a heti verifier. Tradeoff: an adapter must map its stack's states onto ours,
        and a milestone it cannot observe is reported as not measured, never inferred. Daniel
        confirmed, 2026-10-15 (Q2).
      children:
        Every number is labelled with how it was observed, and on which clock = decision:
          nid: 7vsky4sk
          why: >
            Three methods: "observed" is the harness's monotonic clock at a boundary it controls;
            "polled" is the harness clock when a poll first saw a state, with the poll interval as
            its resolution; "reported" is a timestamp the stack wrote, usable only with its clock
            domain and an offset measured during the run. imbu's async operation resource (imbu tick
            ~73tw, Q-EVAG) yields "reported" timestamps, not harness-observed ones. An interval across
            clock domains with no measured offset is not measured. Rejected a two-way outside/inside
            label (the first draft), because it let stack-written timestamps pass as harness-measured
            (maintainability review MNT-3, testability review TST-2, 2026-10-15).
          children:
            The harness clock is the host's CLOCK_MONOTONIC, named by boot id; wall clocks are offset-measured = decision:
              nid: 98rxw488
              why: >-
                Harness events use time.monotonic_ns(), whose clock domain is named monotonic@<boot_id>. On Linux CLOCK_MONOTONIC is one clock for every process on the host since boot, so a stack process on the same box that stamps with it (the summit harness's Registrar publisher does) shares the harness's domain exactly, with zero offset and nothing to measure. A stack that stamps wall time is in domain realtime@<host>; the harness measures that domain's offset by bracketing a realtime read between two monotonic reads at the start and the end of every run, and the bound carried into each interval is the wider bracket plus the change in offset between the two samples, so an NTP step during the run widens the bound instead of hiding in it. A stack on another host needs its own offset source (a time route sampled the same way) and is not measured without one. Rejected wall time as the harness clock, because it steps and slews under NTP and an interval across a step is wrong by the step. Rejected assuming same-host wall clocks agree with zero bound, because that is exactly the unmeasured offset 7vsky4sk forbids.
        Independent verification means a verifier that is not the issuer's code path = decision:
          nid: 27mjdj7q
          why: >
            Utah's pair 3 separates generating a proof from independently verifying it. The verifier
            role is configured separately and the report names it. For imbu that is a separate heti
            process, and ideally also a keripy-based one, since heti is imbu's own substrate.
        Adapters are in-process Python classes per role, declaring before they emit = decision:
          nid: 2vu4969q
          why: >-
            Each role adapter is a Python class with a static declaration (role, the milestones it emits with each one's method, clock and resolution, a versioned knob schema, the stack versions it was contract-tested against), an async start that receives the harness's run context and configured peer endpoints and returns the endpoints it serves, an async stop, and the role's operations (issue, revoke, present, verify, status, sync) as async methods. Every event goes through the context's emit, which refuses a milestone, method or clock the declaration does not name, so the contract suite can check an adapter by running it rather than by reading it. Rejected out-of-process adapters behind a wire protocol: they would let a KERIA team write theirs in TypeScript, but they put one more process and one more clock boundary inside every measured interval and add a protocol to version, while every stack under test is reached over HTTP anyway, so a Python adapter can drive any of them. Rejected post-hoc adapters that scrape a stack's logs into a timeline after the run: an undeclared or misclocked event would be found only after the run, and the open-loop driver needs a live call to schedule. Tradeoff: adapter authors write Python, and an adapter's own overhead sits inside observed intervals; the proxy's pass-through cost is reported, the adapter's is bounded by the calibration stack using the same code paths.
          children:
            An adapter may report another role's milestone it can see, under the owning role = decision:
              nid: 0ys878gy
              why: >-
                imbu's async operation resource reports witnessed and published, which belong to the witness-pool and state-distribution roles, and the issuer adapter is the only one that can read it. So a declaration may name any milestone, but every event is filed under the milestone's owning role and names the adapter that reported it, and the report's observability matrix says which adapter observed each milestone and how. Rejected forcing each role's adapter to observe its own milestones directly: on imbu the witness pool would have to poll witnesses for receipts and correlate them to credentials, a second and coarser measurement of a time imbu already records where it happens. Rejected filing the event under the reporting adapter's role: a timeline whose 'witnessed' sometimes belongs to the issuer cannot be compared across stacks. Tradeoff: two adapters may both declare one milestone; the run refuses that, so each milestone has exactly one source per run.
    It measures a configured deployment, not the software in the abstract = goal:
      nid: 6f6he77q
      why: >
        Registrar window (default 900 s), witness count and toad, Observer gap grace and resync,
        verifier freshness bound and the number of homes are deployment policy, and they dominate
        some results: with defaults, revocation-to-recognition mostly measures the Registrar window.
        So every knob is part of a run's identity and scenarios sweep them; the harness never picks a
        "right" value. Rejected one headline number per pair, because it would silently report the
        defaults. Tradeoff: more runs. Daniel confirmed, 2026-10-15 (Q3).
      children:
        A scenario must set every policy knob explicitly = decision:
          nid: 4q4540c4
          why: >
            An unset knob is an error, not a default, so a report can never rest on a value nobody
            chose. Tradeoff: verbose scenario files.
        ? On the imbu stack, N homes are N imbu issuers sharing one deployment's witnesses, Registrar, Observer, holder and verifier, and K clients are K controllers per home = decision
        : nid: 2lkqz9mq
          why: >-
            KERI-006 names concurrency, and the test plan measures it as K concurrent clients per home and N homes (docs/test-plan.md, KERI-006's named areas). An imbu home is one issuing party: its own lockbox, issuer AID, operation store and control plane, in its own process, so N homes are N such processes, and credentials are dealt to them round-robin. They share the witness pool, the Registrar, the Observer, the holder and the verifier, because that is how SEDI would scale issuance: more issuers against one state-distribution service, which is what N homes is meant to load. A client is a controller: a distinct key imbu authorises, signing its own requests over its own connection pool, and each home's credentials are dealt to its K clients round-robin, so K varies how many independent callers one home serves while the open-loop schedule fixes the total load. Both are knobs of the imbu issuer adapter, homes and clients, so they are part of a run's identity (6f6he77q) and a scenario must set them (4q4540c4). Rejected one issuer adapter per home in the scenario: the harness takes one adapter per role, and a role that is really N processes is the adapter's business, as the witness pool already is. Rejected separate witness pools per home: it would measure N copies of one home, not a shared deployment under N times the load. Tradeoff: per-home numbers come from the timeline's home attribute rather than separate reports.
        ? bulk-steady drives imbu's SEDI proofed bulk ceremony, with a citizens process holding the proofing agent and the SMAIDs, and the Utah-root agent credential issued from imbu's own home = decision
        : nid: 4f987y88
          why: >-
            bulk-steady (test plan, KERI-006's named areas) needs imbu's only bulk route, /v1/imbu/bulk-issuances, which is the SEDI proofed ceremony (imbu @43v3ttq8): a citizen's SEDI Management AID (SMAID), a configured controller, presents an IAR the proofing agent signed for it, after anchoring the IAR's nonce in its own KEL; imbu verifies both, reserves 2(C+1) registries from a pool provisioned ahead, and issues the management Core and Residence pair plus C copy pairs, each copy to a distinct holder AID, every credential in a registry of its own. A spike on 2026-10-21 ran it through imbu ccc8f6c's HTTP app: 3 copies, 8 credentials, 6.4 s. So a bulk issuer adapter, imbu.bulk_issuer, starts a citizens process (keri_bench.stacks.citizens) before imbu, holding the proofing agent and K SMAIDs in lockboxes of their own on the run's witnesses; for each request it has the citizens process mint an IAR and anchor its nonce, then posts the ceremony to imbu itself, each request and poll signed by the SMAID through that process, with the IAR's SAID as the idempotency key. Copy holders are fresh keys, because issuing needs only a distinct AID and the baseline measures issuance; nothing is delivered. The pool is provisioned before the window by the operator, at a size the scenario sets, so registry inception and credential construction are measured apart, which is the split Utah's first pair asks for and the plain route cannot show. The Utah agent credential that every Core's utahAgent edge names is issued at the home's first start by a second facet in imbu's own lockbox, so imbu reads that far node's registry locally; a deployment whose agent credential lives on another issuer's registry adds one far-Observer read per ceremony, which this does not measure and the report says. The Core, Residence and IAR schemas are byte copies of the summit harness's (bakobo/schema 06b60f02, Sam Smith's bytes at keripy 927c0cb8), and the citizen is Sam's Guy. A request is one ceremony: its events name the ceremony, and credentials per second are ceremonies per second times 2(C+1). Rejected standing up a second Registrar and Observer for a separate Utah-root party: imbu must be given its far Observer at start, before the state-distribution role's Observer exists, and the harness's roles start in one order. Tradeoff: the IAR, the anchoring and the SMAID's signatures are the citizens process's work, inside every measured interval, as a citizen's wallet's would be.
          children:
            ? "bulk-steady's sizes: 3 copies a ceremony, 2 citizens, one home stepped from 0.05 to 0.2 ceremonies a second and three homes from 0.1 to 0.4, 120 s windows = decision"
            : nid: 9wm4y7g4
              why: >-
                A probe on 2026-10-21 at harness 1341e1d, imbu 8512c1d, one home, 3 copies (8 credentials a ceremony), 2 citizens: 0.1 ceremonies a second kept up with p50 7.3 s from request to published; 0.2 achieved 0.12 with p50 21.8 s; 0.4 collapsed to 0.036 as operations passed their 120 s timeout. Provisioning 200 registries, with three resolutions, took 36.7 s before the window. So one home steps at 0.05, 0.075, 0.1, 0.125, 0.15 and 0.2 ceremonies a second, and three homes at 0.1, 0.2, 0.3 and 0.4; each window is 120 s, so the lowest step still sees six ceremonies, with 20 s warmup and a 180 s drain, three accepted repetitions in up to six attempts. Each home's pool is 300 registries at one home and 200 at three, more than 2 (3 + 1) times the ceremonies its highest step can request in 140 s. Three copies is the summit's demonstration size and keeps a ceremony inside imbu's 1 to 50; two citizens make each home serve more than one SMAID. The poll is 100 ms, since every poll is a request the citizens process signs. The Registrar window, witnesses and timeouts are those of every earlier run. Tradeoff: steps this slow give few ceremonies a run, so p99 rests on few samples, which the report's counts show.
        ? "The imbu issuer adapter v4: it may prefer to wait for an operation's answer instead of polling, it honours a refused answer's Retry-After, imbu's answer budget is a knob, and accepted is imbu's own stamp = decision"
        : nid: 5k8z0pxe
          why: >-
            imbu utah/next 670a3d8 changes what a client of its operations pays and is allowed. Prefer: wait (imbu @8f8e7hkm, landed in 6a2d4d9) lets a mutating request or a read of its operation hold its answer for up to 25 s until the operation settles, so an operation can cost the principal one signed answer instead of one per poll; the second baseline's census put polls at 4 to 6 of the 7 signatures a credential costs. And every controller now has a token-bucket budget of signed answers (imbu @6u3jm9e4, default 2 a second and a burst of 20), past which imbu answers 429 unsigned with Retry-After. So the adapter gains three knobs, all part of a run's identity (6f6he77q). wait_s: n/a polls every poll_ms as before; a number sends Prefer: wait=<wait_s> on the mutating request and on every read of its operation, and reads again at once while the answer is still pending; exactly one of poll_ms and wait_s is n/a, refused otherwise before any process starts. answer_rate and answer_burst: the budget each home's Service is built with, so a run says which budget it measured and a census can be taken with the budget out of the way. On a 429 the adapter waits the Retry-After it was given and asks again with the same request and idempotency key, within the operation timeout, and counts the refusal in the census beside the signed answers, since a refusal is the one answer imbu does not sign; a 429 without a readable Retry-After is refused as before. accepted becomes a reported milestone read from imbu's own stamp (imbu operations.py stamps it when it stores the operation) in both modes: with a waiting POST the 202 arrives when the operation is done, so the harness-observed 202 would no longer mean acceptance, and one definition across both modes keeps their runs comparable. The PM asked on 2026-10-27 that the targets run measure this imbu, send Prefer: wait, and report signatures per credential with and without it. The Registrar moves with it to bakobo/witness ed1dcfc (witness @23vjzsf0), since imbu 670a3d8 publishes a registry's chain without the issuer KEL (imbu @7qgv63f5) and the Registrar the baselines pinned, 4522151, refuses such a publication with 400. Rejected waiting on reads only: the POST would still be answered once pending and once done, 4 signatures a credential instead of 3, and imbu-prj's choice is a client that waits on both. Rejected leaving the budget at imbu's default with no knob: a census without waiting would then measure the budget's refusals rather than what polling costs, and the reader could not tell which. Tradeoff: accepted now carries imbu's clock offset bound instead of being exact, and timelines before v4 call it observed, which the report shows per run.
          children:
            ? The imbu issuer adapter v5 asks a saturated home again after its 503's Retry-After, as it does a 429, and writes each home's answer counts beside the run = decision
            : nid: 7kmhh4wr
              why: >-
                imbu 44309be (imbu @66fmushs) holds each home's anchoring backlog to what it finished over the last 30 s, at least 32 operations, and refuses a new request past that with 503 e.env.saturated.r and a Retry-After in whole seconds, at least 1, saying when the backlog will fit at the measured rate. An accepted request under its Idempotency-Key is never refused. A client that treated the 503 as final would fail every credential offered while the home is full, and a Drivers License Division's offices would ask again, so the adapter does what it does for the answer budget's 429 (5k8z0pxe): it waits the Retry-After and sends the same request with the same key, signed afresh, until the operation's deadline, operation_timeout_s from the first send; a wait that would end past the deadline is the operation's timeout, e.env.imbu.operation.r, as with a 429. The time spent waiting stays inside requested to witnessed, because requested is the driver's scheduled time, so the latency the targets are judged on includes it. Only 429 and 503 are retried, a 503 only when it is one application/problem+json document whose one code is e.env.saturated.r (a body of another media type, one naming its code twice, or one nested too deep to parse names no code; land review p2.1-p2.3), and either only with a Retry-After the adapter can read as whole ASCII seconds of at most six digits; any other 5xx, a 503 with another code, or a refusal without a readable Retry-After is a refusal, e.party.imbu.refused.f, because a retry the server did not ask for would hide a fault behind a timeout (land review p1.1, p1.2). imbu never says zero, so a Retry-After of 0 is waited as 1 s, which bounds the retries to one a second for the operation's deadline (p1.3). The two refusals are counted apart, the 429s as the controller's budget and the 503s as the home's saturation, and each home's signed, budget-refused and saturation-refused answers and its accepted issuances are written to imbu-answers.json in the run directory when the adapter stops, beside imbu/ rather than in it, because imbu/ is the store whose bytes are the stock footprint. They go to a file and not the census because the census is the external signer's, and a lockbox run, which the volume targets are, has none. The adapter's version is 5. Rejected retrying a 503 at a fixed interval: imbu computes when the backlog fits, and asking sooner only adds refusals.
              children:
                ? The imbu issuer adapter v6 reports constructed and registry_updated from imbu 317ae49, and skips them where imbu leaves them null = decision
                : nid: 2wventxp
                  why: >-
                    One issuer's ceiling of about 1.4 to 1.6 credentials a second sat in imbu's accepted to anchored stage, which imbu did not split, so the bench reported both milestones of Utah's first pair as unobserved (docs/adapters.md). Daniel approved asking imbu to stamp them (Q-RH68, 2026-11-19), and imbu-stamps landed them on imbu utah/next at 317ae49 (bakobo/imbu#47, pinning heti 12e6f41, bakobo/heti#73). As imbu defines them: constructed is when heti had built the credential's ACDC and it passed its schema check; registry_updated is the timestamp heti wrote into the blinded update that commits the credential to issued in its own registry, the TEL event's own time; both precede the KEL anchor, in the same RFC 3339 microsecond form, and a bulk ceremony stamps the latest of each across its credentials, as it does anchored. So the stacks extra moves to imbu 317ae49 and heti 12e6f41, and the adapter, now version 6, reports both as reported realtime milestones for issuance and the bulk ceremony. An issuance imbu adopted after an interruption stamps neither, so for those two alone a null is skipped rather than refused: the credential still has every milestone imbu observed, and refusing would fail every crash run's resumed credential for a stamp that was never owed. Every other owed milestone still refuses a null. Rejected inferring the two from the anchor or accept times: an inferred milestone is never reported (7wt73dlu). Tradeoff: between the two stamps imbu-stamps reports heti's argon2 blind and heti's wait for an earlier act still short of toad, so constructed to registry_updated is not pure CPU under concurrency; the run reading it has to say so. imbu-stamps' profile (imbu .ignored/issuance-cpu-profile-2026-11.md) puts 0.49 of 0.575 s of issuer CPU per credential in argon2, which is Daniel's open question Q-RV5F there.
                  children:
                    ? The stacks extra moves to imbu main 7b544ac and the heti it pins, 093acb3, where a credential pays argon2 once and the issue-side blind runs on a thread pool = decision
                    : nid: 06klkn9m
                      why: >-
                        imbu-stamps' CPU profile (imbu .ignored/issuance-cpu-profile-2026-11.md, imbu 192099d with heti cfb381d) put 0.49 of 0.575 s of issuer CPU per credential in argon2 crypto_pwhash, called twice per credential, which caps one issuer near 1.75 credentials a second and matches every run's 1.4 to 1.6 ceiling. Daniel answered Q-RV5F there: run argon2 once per credential. heti#74 (125180b) has a grant reuse the blinder heti kept at issue, and heti#75 (093acb3) derives the issue-side blind on a process-wide pool of 4 threads before the issue checks state; imbu main 7b544ac (bakobo/imbu#49) pins that heti. imbu's utah/next was merged into main (bakobo/imbu#48) and deleted, so the pin is on imbu main. Between 317ae49 and 7b544ac imbu changed only src/imbu/container.py; between 12e6f41 and 093acb3 heti changed only its anchored produce path (blinding, credentials, ipex, registries), not Witnesses.start or open_witness_stores, so the witness subclass and the adapter need no change and the adapter stays at v6. The pin moves so the bench can measure the fix: the rerun is Daniel's instruction of 2026-11-19. Consequence for reading a run: constructed and registry_updated still bracket the blind, which now runs before heti's _settle_prior, so constructed to registry_updated includes the pool wait plus one argon2 pass; and a client polling faster than imbu's per-controller answer budget gets 429 sooner, because the loop is no longer blocked. Rejected pinning heti utah/next directly without an imbu that pins it: imbu's lock would resolve a different heti than the one it was tested with.
                      children:
                        ? The argon2 fix is measured by probe-capacity-1home at both pins in one quiet window, with 3.5 and 4.0 a second added by --rate = decision
                        : nid: 279zu9ps
                          why: >-
                            No probe had run at the old pin, imbu 317ae49 with heti 12e6f41; probe-capacity-1home's only result is October's at imbu 2c3b072, before imbu bounded its anchoring backlog (44309be), so it could not serve as the before. So the same harness ran the scenario at both pins back to back on kelun on 2026-11-20, new pin first, each calibrated under the strict gate, holding the shared bench-host flock that the busy sessions on kelun agreed to honour, and starting only once the one-minute load fell under 2. The scenario's rates stop at 2.8, the peak-hour target, and arithmetic from imbu-stamps' profile put the new ceiling near 3, so a run that kept up at 2.8 would not have located it; 3.5 and 4.0 were added as single runs with keri-bench run --rate, which leaves the scenario and its hash unchanged. Rejected editing the scenario's rates, which would change its hash and break comparison with October. Tradeoff: each step is one 60 s window, a probe, so keeping up at a rate shows short-window capacity, not that the rate holds for an hour; the peak-hour scenario is what tests that.
                          children:
                            ? 'One issuer gets the full peak hour, peak-hour-1home-hour: peak-hour-3homes at one issuer, run with the argon2 follow-ups after midnight = decision'
                            : nid: 3s6zgux4
                              why: >-
                                peak-hour-1home is ten minutes because one issuer failed 2.8 a second from its first minute on every earlier pin (9cuqzk0u). At imbu 7b544ac and heti 093acb3 one issuer kept up with 4.0 a second in one-minute probes (docs/argon2-2026-11.md), so whether it carries Utah's peak hour is now an open question, and ten minutes cannot answer it. So peak-hour-1home-hour is peak-hour-3homes with homes = 1 and nothing else changed: 2.8 a second until 10,000 credentials are witnessed or the hour ends, every 101st delivered, verified and revoked, so it reads directly against the three-issuer verdict in docs/targets-2026-10-rerun.md. Rejected lengthening peak-hour-1home, which would change that scenario's hash and its comparison with October. Daniel asked on 2026-11-20 that the follow-ups (this scenario, the ten-minute one, a probe of one issuer above 4.0 and the three-issuer probe) run after midnight, when kelun is idle; they run under the shared bench-host flock, start only once the one-minute load is under 2, and give up if it is not by 05:00 local. Tradeoff: about two and a half hours of the host's night.
    One box by default, with real faults at the application boundary, and honest about the rest = constraint:
      nid: 0wcr2rrj
      why: >
        The harness owns every inter-role link through a proxy it controls (delay, drop/partition,
        duplicate) and owns process lifecycle (SIGKILL and restart), so faults are reproducible and
        timestamped in the timeline. That is faithful to KERI's HTTP/CESR failure behaviour, but it
        is not a kernel-level partition and not many hosts; the report says which kind of run it was.
        Rejected container networking with tc/netem as the base, because it adds a dependency Utah
        must reproduce and cannot duplicate a message. Multi-host is a later deployment of the same
        design. Consequence: adapters must accept configured endpoints. Daniel agreed, 2026-10-15 (Q4).
      children:
        The fault proxy is an HTTP/1.1-aware reverse proxy per endpoint that never rewrites bytes = decision:
          nid: 6wlc25pz
          why: >-
            Every link between roles on the stacks under test is HTTP/1.1 (witnesses, the Registrar, the Observer, imbu's control plane), and the duplicate fault must replay one whole message, which a byte-level TCP proxy cannot delimit. So the harness puts one asyncio reverse proxy in front of every endpoint an adapter announces, reads each message whole within size limits, and applies delay, partition (requests are swallowed and their clients get no answer until the partition heals) and duplicate (an identical copy on a fresh upstream connection, its answer discarded). It rewrites nothing, not even Host, because clients sign @authority under RFC 9421 and the upstream checks it against the Host it receives. Rejected a TCP proxy with byte-offset faults, because it cannot duplicate a message. Rejected an off-the-shelf proxy (toxiproxy), because it adds a binary Utah must reproduce and still works at the byte level. Tradeoff: buffering adds about 0.2 ms at p50 and under 1 ms at p99 per hop on one host, which every report states per link; upgrades, 100-continue and HTTP/2 are not carried.
          children:
            The proxy ends a client's connection when the upstream ends it, not when a header says it will = decision:
              nid: 2q4mw7cs
              why: >-
                Found running real keripy witnesses behind the proxy, 2026-10-15: a witness answers Connection: close and keeps the socket open, and keripy's client sends its next request, the other witnesses' receipts, down that same socket. A proxy that honoured the header closed the connection, the receipts never arrived, every witness held the issuer's inception in escrow, and the issuer's OOBI answered 404, so nothing downstream could start. The proxy now watches the upstream between requests and closes the client's side only when the upstream closes or speaks unasked, which is what a client talking to the server directly would see. Rejected reconnecting upstream and retrying the next request, because that would hide a real close from the client and change the failure behaviour under test. Tradeoff: one extra pending read per idle connection.
        A crashed real-stack process restarts from what it had on disk, and each witness is a process of its own = decision:
          nid: 5ed29j5w
          why: >-
            Restart recovery is one of KERI-006's named areas, and the test plan reports time to ready and time to first new success after a crash on each role. A restart that rebuilt a role from nothing would measure re-provisioning instead: a fresh witness has a new AID nobody's KEL names, a fresh holder has a new AID nobody issued to. So every process the imbu stack starts keeps its state where a real deployment would, under the run directory, and reopens it when restarted: imbu reopens its home, each witness reopens its keystore with the same salt, and the holder and verifier reopen their lockboxes. The holder keeps which credential is which on disk too, and after a restart presents a held credential from heti's retained copy and the Observer's current chain, since the bench wrapper's in-memory index is not heti's and losing it would charge the wrapper's amnesia to heti. Each witness runs in its own process, named witness_pool.w<i>, and a crash fault may name one process of a role, so a run can kill one witness of three at toad 2, which is the redundancy a witness pool exists for, as well as the whole pool. Rejected crashing only whole roles: killing all three witnesses at once tests only the case the pool is designed to avoid. Tradeoff: the witnesses' keystores persist for the run, so a run directory is larger, and a witness process per witness costs memory per witness.
    The HSM question is answered by sensitivity, through a real signer seam = decision:
      nid: 4jlx9k8q
      why: >
        An HSM rarely makes one signature faster; it moves signing off the box, adds latency per
        signature and caps the rate. So the instrument reports a signature census per role per
        lifecycle, a primitive microbenchmark, and end-to-end curves while a latency- and rate-capped
        signer is swept across HSM profiles. Daniel chose (2026-10-15, Q5) to build that signer as an
        implementation of a real external-signer interface in heti (plan item heti-signer, heti tick
        ~2ppq) rather than a test-only monkeypatch of keripy's Manager.sign, so the same seam later
        takes a real HSM. On KERIA the seam is Signify's ExternalModule. Tradeoff: bench-baseline
        waits on heti-signer.
      children:
        ? The primitive microbenchmark times keripy's own Ed25519 and Blake3-256 on the host, one core, in its own process = decision
        : nid: 9n80j7ph
          why: >-
            The test plan's HSM section asks for a microbenchmark of Ed25519 sign and verify and Blake3-256 on the host, so a reader can set the end-to-end curves against what one signature costs here. It times keripy's Signer.sign, Verfer.verify and Diger with Blake3_256, the calls the stack under test makes, over fixed payload sizes, in a process of its own under the same nice level as the runs, and reports per-operation p50 and p99 and operations per second, with the host fingerprint. Rejected timing libsodium or the blake3 package directly: the cost the stack pays includes keripy's CESR wrapping, and that is what an HSM would replace. Tradeoff: one core's numbers, not a multi-core aggregate; the doc says so.
        ? hsm-sweep signs imbu's principal through heti's ThrottledSigner in imbu's own process, instrumented, and the census and the profile check come from that signer = decision
        : nid: 2p26rn9h
          why: >-
            imbu ccc8f6c can mint its principal with an external signer and attaches one on every start (imbu @42n54pc7); heti f568f7e ships SoftwareSigner, deterministic from a salt and a key index, and ThrottledSigner, which makes any signer behave like an HSM with a latency per operation and a cap on operation starts per second (heti @5dmsz9h5). So the imbu issuer adapter gains a signer knob: lockbox keeps the principal's keys in the lockbox as before, and external mints and attaches a ThrottledSigner over a SoftwareSigner whose salt is fixed for the run, so a restart attaches the same keys. Its HsmProfile is a subclass that records, for every operation, when it claimed its start and when its latency ended, on the host's monotonic clock, which is the harness's clock domain (98rxw488); the issuer process serves those records on a loopback port, and the adapter reads them once the drain ends into provenance, from which the report derives the census and the profile check. The census counts the signer's operations by kind and sets them against what the timeline implies: imbu signs every answer to an authenticated request (imbu src/imbu/control/signing.py), so every operation poll costs a signature, and the adapter counts the requests it made; what remains, per credential issued, is the KEL and exchange signing, which the stack test pins. The profile check (test plan, HSM sensitivity) refuses a run unless the median measured latency is within 5% of the configured one (or under 1 ms when none is configured), and, when the cap was ever the reason an operation waited, the median spacing of those starts is within 5% of 1/rate; a cap that never bound is reported as not exercised, not as a pass. Rejected a signer in its own process behind HTTP: the profile's latency already stands for the trip to a device, and a second hop would add a cost no profile names. Rejected counting signatures by patching keripy (Daniel's Q5, 2026-10-15). Tradeoff: the signer shares imbu's event loop, so a profile's latency is honoured only as well as that loop is free, which is exactly what the 5% check is there to catch.
          children:
            ? 'The hsm-sweep grid: no added latency, then 15 to 120 ms per signature, and at 30 ms caps of 10 and 5 starts a second, each stepped to saturation at one home = decision'
            : nid: 02fe3uk5
              why: >-
                The brief asks for a grid that spans the range a cloud KMS such as AWS KMS would plausibly fall in, without calibrating against one (Daniel's Q-SNJA). A signature from a cloud KMS in the same region is a network round trip plus the device's time, so tens of milliseconds, with a tail past 100 ms; so the latency points are 15, 30, 60 and 120 ms, uncapped, beside the external signer with no added latency, which is the same code path as an on-box HSM's best case and the comparator for the rest. Points below 15 ms were measured and cannot be honoured on this host: imbu's own event loop wakes the profile's timer 0.2 to 0.6 ms late, which is more than 5% of 5 ms, so the profile check would refuse every run; the no-latency point stands for that end of the range. A cap is a device's limit on operation starts, and a KMS's request quotas are per account rather than per key, so caps are swept at the one latency, 30 ms, at 10 and 5 starts a second, which bind at this stack's rates: on imbu ccc8f6c a credential costs 2 signatures plus one per answer to an authenticated request, so with the adapter's 50 ms poll about 7 signatures per credential, and a cap of 10 holds one home near 1.4 credentials a second. Each profile steps offered load at 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1 and 1.5 per second, 60 s each, 10 s warmup, 120 s drain, up to six attempts for three accepted repetitions, issuance only, measured requested to witnessed as the test plan names. One more curve runs three homes at 30 ms uncapped, at 1 to 4 per second, since each home's principal has its own signer. The poll interval stays at 50 ms, the interval of every earlier run, and the census reports what the polls cost, because a client's poll rate is a deployment choice the report should expose rather than tune away. Rejected a full latency-by-cap cross product: about 30 profiles, more than a day of this host, for curves the one-dimensional sweeps already bound. Tradeoff: no point between the no-latency signer and 15 ms.
            The simulated HSM keeps its schedule on a thread and event loop of its own, and imbu awaits its answer = decision:
              nid: 0v8kmser
              why: >-
                The first pass of hsm-sweep (2026-10-21, harness 2b65364) ran the profile's schedule on imbu's own event loop, and imbu's loop, busy with issuance, woke the device's timers 1.5 to 2 ms late: at 30 ms the signer's median measured 31.2 to 32.1 ms, 4 to 7% over, and the profile check refused most runs. A real HSM's latency does not depend on imbu's loop; what imbu's loop decides is when imbu notices the answer, and that belongs in the end-to-end numbers, not in the device's. So RecordingProfile runs heti's HsmProfile schedule, its start claims and its latency, as a coroutine on a dedicated asyncio loop in a daemon thread, which does nothing else and so wakes on time, and stamps requested, started and finished there; imbu awaits the result through a thread-safe future. The profile check then tests the device, and any lateness of imbu's own loop shows up where it is imbu's, in requested-to-witnessed. That also lifts the floor the grid set at 15 ms (02fe3uk5), so the grid gains a 5 ms point. Rejected subtracting a measured overrun from the device's sleep: it would tune the device to hide imbu's loop, and the overrun varies with load. Tradeoff: one thread per signer in the issuer process, and the hand-off adds a few microseconds per operation.
              children:
                'The 5 ms point still cannot pass: an idle device loop wakes 0.34 to 0.40 ms late on this host = decision':
                  nid: 63txu3fm
                  why: >-
                    The decision above expected a device loop of its own to lift the 15 ms floor (02fe3uk5), and added a 5 ms point. Run on 2026-10-21 at harness a4fc364, the device's median latency was 15.38 to 15.71 ms at 15 ms, 30.35 to 30.85 at 30, 60.63 to 60.95 at 60 and 120.90 to 121.02 at 120, all within 3%, but 5.34 to 5.40 ms at 5, 7 to 8% over, so the profile check refused all 30 runs of that profile. The lateness is the operating system's wake-up of a nice-19 thread and asyncio's millisecond timeouts, not imbu's loop: it is the same 0.35 to 0.9 ms at every latency. So the floor stands at 15 ms, for this reason rather than the one 02fe3uk5 gave, and the refused 5 ms runs are kept as the record of it. Rejected loosening the 5% check for short profiles: the check is the test plan's, and a profile it cannot confirm is one no curve should be drawn for. Rejected a busy-waiting device: it would take a core from the stack it measures.
    ? The docs, the write-ups and the committed reports are published on a static site, built as the other bakobo suites' are = decision
    : nid: 2j423x8t
      why: >-
        The repository went public on 2026-11-17, and its readers had only markdown on GitHub: the write-ups under docs/ cite run directories by path, and a reader has to know which of 422 report.md files under results/ backs which claim. Daniel asked the same day for a GitHub Pages site in the style and rough model of keri-conformance-suite's (5s5wqrcp there), and pointed at keri-priv-analysis-suite's, built that morning on the same model (4vdpvhcz there). Chose that site's shape outright so the three read as one family: Zensical pinned exactly because it is pre-1.0, the keri.one theme, and a script that assembles plain markdown into _site/src (the README as the home page, docs/ beside it, and every committed .md under results/), rewrites links for that layout, sends a link to any other repository file to GitHub, and refuses a link to a path that does not exist, a rendered page with markup outside a short allow-list, and active content in a published SVG; a Pages workflow builds that tree with a read-only token and deploys it from a job that runs no repository code, and CI builds the same site on every change so a dead link fails before the deploy. The site renders only what is committed and never runs a scenario: each report.md is the one keri-bench report wrote from its timeline and provenance, so the site cannot disagree with a report a reader regenerates. Rejected copying the assembler as a shared package: two suites own one each today, and a shared one is worth it at the third, not before. Rejected publishing the timelines, provenance and JSON reports as pages: they are inputs for a reader's own recomputation, so the site links them on GitHub. Tradeoff: the assembler's code and tests are a near copy of keri-priv-analysis-suite's, so a fix to one has to be carried to the other by hand.
      children:
        A reader who has never heard of KERI can find out what each page says, without the committed reports changing = decision:
          nid: 5gwu92w5
          why: >-
            Daniel's test for keri-priv-analysis-suite's site, the same day (his words to that session): "a human could browse this site and understand what's being reported. I helped design the system, and if I can't even understand it, it is way, way, way too opaque." He asked for it to be taken into account here. A naive read of this site (DeepSeek told it knew nothing of KERI, fixed questions, 2026-11-17) found the write-ups readable, the peak-hour verdict answered at full confidence, and everything around them opaque: "home" and "witness" defined nowhere, about twenty terms it listed as not understood (KEL, TEL, SEDI, imbu, heti, Registrar, Observer, toad, Prefer: wait and the error codes among them), and a run report that is the harness's raw output, durations in integer nanoseconds, with no way back to the write-up it is evidence for, while 422 such pages are reachable through the site's search. So the site gains a reader layer and the reports stay as they are: a glossary, a page on reading a run report, a home page that starts with what was found, and a short note the assembler puts at the top of every published run report, naming the run, the write-up that cites it, how to read it and the glossary. The note is the site's, set apart as an admonition, and the report's own text follows it unchanged, so the committed report and its page still say the same thing. A run's write-up is the one in docs/ that cites its results directory, preferring the one whose name the directory's starts with. Rejected rewriting keri-bench report to print milliseconds and a summary and regenerating the 422 reports: their bytes are covered by committed SHA256SUMS manifests that the write-ups cite as evidence, and changing what was measured-and-recorded to change how it reads is the wrong trade; a reader-friendlier report format is a separate change for future runs. Rejected a model comprehension gate: keri-priv-analysis-suite built one with a control that withholds the page, and it could not tell readable pages from opaque ones (6r9sjucj there), because a model reads jargon a person cannot; a model's answers show only that a fact is on the page. That is all the naive read here was used for, to find terms the pages never define, and it is no evidence that a person finds the site readable. Tradeoff: the glossary and the reading guide are hand-written and can drift from the report format; a test holds the guide's section names to the ones the report renders.
          children:
            The generic pages say issuer where imbu says home, and lead with the test plan's roles = decision:
              nid: 0wexjj45
              why: >-
                Reading the published site on 2026-11-18, Daniel objected that "home" is heti and imbu's word, not KERI's, on pages meant to be generic to the KERI ecosystem, and suggested "wallet". In keri-bench a home is one imbu issuing process with its own store, issuing identifier and KEL (docs/adapters.md, the homes knob), so it is on the issuing side; the wallet in these runs is the holder. Proposed "issuer", which is the ACDC specification's term for the party that issues and the test plan's role name, and Daniel chose it (Q-P3R4). So the write-ups, the README and the test plan say issuer (one issuer, three issuers, per issuer), the imbu binding in docs/adapters.md says once that imbu calls an issuer instance a home, and identifiers keep their spelling (scenario and results names such as office-day-1home, the homes knob, process names such as issuer.h0), with the glossary saying what 1home and 3homes mean. The same sweep leads with the test plan's generic roles (issuer, holder, verifier, witness pool, state distribution) and names imbu, heti, the Registrar and the Observer where the specific implementation matters. this.i itself is not rewritten: its whys are the record of what was decided when, in the words used then. Rejected "wallet": it names the holder's side, so it would have reversed what every throughput finding is about. Rejected renaming the knob and the scenarios: they are inputs that committed results and their checksums cite.
            Every term is defined once, and shown on hover wherever it appears unless it fills every page = decision:
              nid: 4wnur86k
              why: >-
                Daniel asked whether the site had copied keri-priv-analysis-suite's moves, hover text and terms linked to their definitions among them, and chose to add them (Q-Z6AF). The glossary moves into one data file, docs/glossary.toml: each term's name, the words a reader will meet it as, its section and its meaning, with the milestones defined as the test plan defines them. scripts/glossary writes docs/glossary.md from it, and a test fails when the committed page differs from what the data produces, so the page a reader browses on GitHub and the data cannot drift. The site assembler appends an abbreviation definition for every spelling of every term to every page it publishes, reports included, so the site renders each occurrence with its meaning on hover (Python-Markdown's abbr extension, added to the site's extensions and to the assembler's markup check together). Rejected writing hover text into each page: a definition repeated in thirty places drifts in thirty ways. Tradeoff: abbr matches whole words and is case-sensitive, so a term is defined for the spellings listed and no others, and a common word listed as a term shows its meaning everywhere it appears; so the words that fill every page (issuer, holder, verifier and the other roles, fault, soak) carry no hover spelling, since an underline under each would bury the hovers that help (Copilot on the pull request asked for them, and was declined), and the glossary page defines them all.
            Run reports are rendered for people, and the committed ones are re-rendered from their report.json = decision:
              nid: 6d0jwr9z
              why: >-
                This reverses one rejection in 5gwu92w5, on Daniel's choice of 2026-11-18 (Q-Z6AF), as keri-priv-analysis-suite did for its own reports the same day. A run's report.md now opens with a summary in sentences (what was offered, how the measured interval came out at the median and the 99th percentile, whether the system kept up, what a fault did, what became of every credential), gives every duration in the unit a person reads (ms, s) rather than integer nanoseconds, and gives clock readings as times into the run rather than readings of an arbitrary clock. report.json is unchanged and keeps exact nanoseconds. The 422 committed report.md files are re-rendered by the new renderer from each run's committed report.json, never re-derived from the timeline, because derive() has changed since many of those runs and re-deriving could move a number a write-up cites; rendering the JSON that was committed changes how a number reads and never which number it is. Each results directory's SHA256SUMS is updated for its report.md lines only, and a test holds that every other line, timelines, provenance and report.json among them, is byte-identical to before. The earlier rejection rested on the manifests' bytes being evidence; the evidence is the timeline and provenance, which no line of this change touches, and report.md was always derived from them. Tradeoff: a report.md's header still names the generator commit recorded in its report.json, which is not the code that rendered the markdown; the summary says it was rendered from report.json.
    The repository is open source under Apache-2.0, held by Bakobo LLC = decision:
      nid: 8y4sz8tl
      why: >-
        keri-bench went public on 2026-11-17 with the template's Apache-2.0 text sitting at the root as oss-LICENSE since the initial commit, which GitHub already reported as the repository's licence although nobody had chosen it, and the template's all-rights-reserved COPYRIGHT.md beside it. The public posture standard makes the licensing position a stated business decision, never a default (dev/standards/oss-posture.md item 1). Daniel chose Apache-2.0, open source, on 2026-11-18 (Q-RNYS). It fits the repository's first goal (4y4rrghd): Utah, and anyone else, must be able to rerun the same instrument against KERIA or any other implementation, which they can do freely only under an open licence. Rejected published but not open: it would have made the licence already shown on a public repository a wrong grant to purge from history, which narrows and cannot recall a grant already received, and it would have forbidden exactly the reruns the instrument exists for. Tradeoff: anyone may fork and change the harness, so a number produced by a modified copy carries no Bakobo provenance; every report names the harness commit that produced it, which is how a reader tells them apart.
